{
  "dom_id": "step-q251826",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14145046/assets/0e95f9087b5b607f2d33d49c0f5db123eede589e53adc94747f3a33c1fe68f90.png)\n\n**From top to bottom**, the **exact** values of the $y$-intercepts of the parabola shown above are{{field-1}}\nand \n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-8,-1)$ and passes through the point $(-6,1).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(-8,-1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y+1)^{2}=4p(x+8).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-6,1),$ we substitute $x=-6$ and $y=1$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+1)^{2} & =4p(-6+8) \\\\ 4 & =4p(2) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =2(x+8).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =2(0+8) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{16} \\\\ y & =\\pm 4-1 \\\\ y & =-5,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are $3$ and  $-5.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
